1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:03,900
What does it take to get the lifeblood of a building flowing?

2
00:00:04,860 --> 00:00:06,500
Try 50 miles of pipe.

3
00:00:07,060 --> 00:00:10,680
Enough wire to stretch from New York City to Washington, D.C.

4
00:00:12,560 --> 00:00:13,820
600 toilets.

5
00:00:15,060 --> 00:00:16,740
And a heck of a lot of solder.

6
00:00:17,840 --> 00:00:20,080
We'll find out how they used to do things.

7
00:00:20,700 --> 00:00:24,660
And we'll see through the structure we're building how these guys work today.

8
00:00:25,720 --> 00:00:27,380
Getting into the flow of things.

9
00:00:27,900 --> 00:00:28,820
Getting wired.

10
00:00:28,821 --> 00:00:30,740
and getting plastered.

11
00:00:31,280 --> 00:00:32,380
So grab a seat.

12
00:00:32,960 --> 00:00:35,500
This show promises to be electrifying.

13
00:00:36,360 --> 00:00:38,420
Now, building a skyscraper,

14
00:00:38,760 --> 00:00:41,000
The Arteries, on Modern Marvels.

15
00:00:58,180 --> 00:01:03,340
For the past 21 months, the builders of the new California Department of Transportation,

16
00:01:03,341 --> 00:01:06,620
or Caltrans headquarters in downtown Los Angeles,

17
00:01:07,360 --> 00:01:16,060
have been lifting, welding, pouring, installing, testing, sweating, and swearing.

18
00:01:16,200 --> 00:01:18,480
Put number two right here.

19
00:01:18,780 --> 00:01:24,480
In an effort to make this massive 750,000 square foot office building a reality.

20
00:01:27,360 --> 00:01:30,920
Much of the innovative design of the building is evident from the outside.

21
00:01:30,921 --> 00:01:44,921
But inside, hidden behind walls and above dropped ceilings, is a labyrinth of pipes and wires connecting every area of the building and making office life possible.

22
00:01:47,440 --> 00:01:50,620
In the industry we say that heating and cooling is a luxury, but plumbing is essential.

23
00:01:52,320 --> 00:01:55,340
The Scott Company is the plumbing contractor on this project.

24
00:01:56,940 --> 00:01:59,520
Its technicians are working on the guts of the building.

25
00:01:59,521 --> 00:02:04,080
the veins and arteries that will finally bring this structure to life.

26
00:02:05,560 --> 00:02:11,560
Without water and drainage, society doesn't exist as we know it.

27
00:02:12,200 --> 00:02:18,000
Disease, everything that's been pestilence on man has since the beginning of time

28
00:02:18,001 --> 00:02:22,980
basically been traced 90% of it back to sanitation.

29
00:02:23,400 --> 00:02:25,240
Without sanitation, you have no cities.

30
00:02:25,241 --> 00:02:30,040
The Scott Company is responsible for installing the plumbing system.

31
00:02:30,860 --> 00:02:34,920
Pipes, sinks, toilets, faucets, and pumps.

32
00:02:37,080 --> 00:02:38,740
Edward Mahoney is the general foreman.

33
00:02:40,260 --> 00:02:43,360
He's here to make sure no one's sitting back taking it easy.

34
00:02:44,980 --> 00:02:49,420
If anyone is, this job could spiral out of control.

35
00:02:50,360 --> 00:02:54,180
Basically, plumbing system consists of two components.

36
00:02:55,240 --> 00:03:02,160
The pressurized water system delivering water to your fixtures, and the drainage system, which takes the wastewater away.

37
00:03:03,040 --> 00:03:09,780
In installing a plumbing system in a building this size, we install the risers going from floor to floor.

38
00:03:10,620 --> 00:03:16,340
Risers are the vertical pipes that move supply water up and wastewater down throughout the building.

39
00:03:16,341 --> 00:03:26,460
In the case of multiple bathrooms, we prefabricate whole batteries of toilets, urinals, whatever, particularly if they're repetitive multiple times.

40
00:03:26,800 --> 00:03:32,380
We bring out a package with the waste and the water already assembled and attached to the risers.

41
00:03:32,760 --> 00:03:38,740
This makes it fast and efficient and then hook it up, test it, have it inspected, and it's done.

42
00:03:39,840 --> 00:03:40,540
That's it.

43
00:03:41,080 --> 00:03:44,280
This is going to be a bathroom upon completion.

44
00:03:44,980 --> 00:03:47,380
We have the wall that's open with all the pipes in it now.

45
00:03:48,040 --> 00:03:54,820
We have the two-inch water line here that will be furnishing the water to the water closets, toilets, and sinks.

46
00:03:55,440 --> 00:03:59,980
And this is the hot water line that will be furnishing hot water to the sinks.

47
00:04:00,760 --> 00:04:03,620
And then eventually there will be a toilet setting right here.

48
00:04:03,621 --> 00:04:07,240
That's the waste in the floor for the toilet.

49
00:04:07,241 --> 00:04:14,240
The Scott Company is installing 700 toilets and urinals and 450 faucets.

50
00:04:14,860 --> 00:04:24,140
It's also running 50 miles of pipe, ranging from half-inch faucet supply lines to massive 12-inch storm drain lines.

51
00:04:27,280 --> 00:04:32,300
The plumbing engineer has to take into consideration many factors when designing a plumbing system.

52
00:04:32,301 --> 00:04:38,680
He starts by looking at the number of fixtures required by the building plans

53
00:04:38,681 --> 00:04:44,720
Next, he determines how much water demand it takes to feed all the fixtures

54
00:04:44,721 --> 00:04:46,480
In gallons per minute

55
00:04:46,481 --> 00:04:48,480
Without pressure, the water doesn't flow

56
00:04:48,481 --> 00:04:52,860
The water flows totally under pressure to get it from one place to the other

57
00:04:52,861 --> 00:04:59,160
In all tall buildings, the city water pressure alone isn't enough to push it to upper floors

58
00:04:59,161 --> 00:05:02,160
Our supply is from the city.

59
00:05:02,161 --> 00:05:05,160
On this particular building, we go up seven floors on city pressure.

60
00:05:05,161 --> 00:05:10,160
And for the upper 8th through 13th floor, it requires a pumping system.

61
00:05:11,160 --> 00:05:13,160
Here we have the house pumps.

62
00:05:13,161 --> 00:05:20,160
These are pumps that appear to be very small, but they do a very good job for the water, servicing the whole entire building.

63
00:05:20,161 --> 00:05:26,160
All floors, all your lavatories, sinks, drinking fountains, your kitchen area.

64
00:05:26,161 --> 00:05:30,000
There are all the water. There's a building that all goes through these pumps.

65
00:05:31,880 --> 00:05:35,800
Each of these pumps is capable of moving about 50 gallons of water per minute.

66
00:05:39,640 --> 00:05:42,060
And this building will need that kind of volume.

67
00:05:42,720 --> 00:05:50,120
A large building like the Caltrans building, for example, might use 15 million gallons of water a year.

68
00:05:50,640 --> 00:05:55,080
It would equate to filling a large NFL football stadium up to the stands with water.

69
00:05:56,160 --> 00:06:02,220
That is a lot of bathroom breaks, but 15 million gallons is half of what a building this size could consume.

70
00:06:06,540 --> 00:06:14,040
Low-flow toilets and automatic faucets will help keep the state of California from flushing resources down the drain.

71
00:06:14,940 --> 00:06:20,220
What's special about these is that they have sensors so that when you wash your hands,

72
00:06:22,180 --> 00:06:24,360
you take your hand away and the water stops running.

73
00:06:24,361 --> 00:06:27,960
It's going to save considerable in the long run.

74
00:06:29,200 --> 00:06:35,700
In semi-arid Los Angeles, water conservation is a primary concern for the designers of the Caltrans project.

75
00:06:37,840 --> 00:06:44,640
This and other structures being built throughout the world are leaning more and more towards sustainable, or green, construction.

76
00:06:46,500 --> 00:06:51,860
Our energy crisis in the United States for a lot of countries around the world is a water crisis.

77
00:06:51,861 --> 00:06:57,860
and green buildings offer a tremendous amount of attention towards water savings.

78
00:06:58,920 --> 00:07:02,220
Making the biggest splash yet in the world of water conservation

79
00:07:02,221 --> 00:07:07,400
will be the 54-story One Bryant Park building going up in New York City.

80
00:07:09,620 --> 00:07:13,580
Designers hope it will be the biggest, greenest skyscraper ever constructed.

81
00:07:15,480 --> 00:07:19,920
Developed by the Durst Organization and designed by Cook Plus Fox Architects,

82
00:07:19,921 --> 00:07:23,660
The building will use what's known as a gray water system.

83
00:07:24,520 --> 00:07:32,220
This site is two acres in area, and each year, on average, four feet of water fall on this site.

84
00:07:32,860 --> 00:07:39,760
We're going to capture all of it, and we're going to use it to flush the toilets and for cooling tower makeup.

85
00:07:40,240 --> 00:07:42,640
And we're going to treat it before we use it.

86
00:07:44,280 --> 00:07:48,920
The captured rainwater will be used in the toilets, but not in the urinals.

87
00:07:49,920 --> 00:07:52,260
The urinals won't use any water at all.

88
00:07:53,260 --> 00:07:58,980
A waterless urinal looks just like any other urinal, except there's no flush valve on it.

89
00:07:59,500 --> 00:08:04,440
And it has a cartridge that is filled with a liquid that is lighter than urine.

90
00:08:05,060 --> 00:08:09,860
So when one urinates into the urinal, the water flows down the sides like any other urinal

91
00:08:09,861 --> 00:08:12,400
and actually flows under the liquid.

92
00:08:12,800 --> 00:08:16,860
It's a blue-colored liquid, harmless to the environment, flows under this liquid,

93
00:08:17,200 --> 00:08:19,860
and right down the trap, down into the sewer system.

94
00:08:19,920 --> 00:08:26,600
That liquid maintains the barrier between the sewer system and your nose.

95
00:08:26,860 --> 00:08:31,620
One day we just sat down and did the math, and at 2 million square feet, if you look at the population,

96
00:08:31,800 --> 00:08:39,520
look at the number of times a urinal is typically flushed, it ends up being about 3 million gallons a year of water, potable water saved.

97
00:08:41,840 --> 00:08:47,740
Although the urinals at the Caltrans building aren't waterless, they only use one gallon per flush.

98
00:08:47,741 --> 00:08:52,500
Most home toilets use three and a half gallons per use

99
00:08:52,501 --> 00:08:57,620
It's estimated the 2,000 occupants of the Caltrans building

100
00:08:57,621 --> 00:09:00,820
Will flush 10,000 gallons of water a day

101
00:09:00,821 --> 00:09:07,700
The plumbing contractor is working to keep things flowing

102
00:09:07,701 --> 00:09:10,780
But something doesn't quite smell right

103
00:09:10,781 --> 00:09:14,100
And it's not coming from the sewer connect

104
00:09:14,101 --> 00:09:17,260
The people working for the Scott Company

105
00:09:17,261 --> 00:09:20,520
are about to be hit with some really bad news.

106
00:09:23,240 --> 00:09:27,500
The Empire State Building originally had 2,500 toilets and sinks

107
00:09:27,501 --> 00:09:30,200
fed by 51 miles of pipe.

108
00:09:31,420 --> 00:09:35,080
Building a skyscraper, the arteries will return on Modern Marvels.

109
00:09:38,460 --> 00:09:42,040
Modern Marvels is brought to you in part by A.G. Edwards.

110
00:09:42,041 --> 00:09:50,080
The development of the skyscraper is the story of technologies coalescing at just the right time

111
00:09:50,081 --> 00:09:52,860
to send structures soaring skyward.

112
00:09:54,660 --> 00:09:55,880
Technologies including

113
00:09:55,881 --> 00:09:59,040
indoor plumbing.

114
00:10:00,780 --> 00:10:06,560
Public water supply is one of these great technological accomplishments of the 19th century.

115
00:10:07,240 --> 00:10:12,880
Without these water supply systems, you could not have the concentrations of population

116
00:10:12,881 --> 00:10:18,440
that you have in the cities and in those little cities that are skyscrapers.

117
00:10:19,360 --> 00:10:23,260
Indoor plumbing in the U.S. actually predates the birth of the skyscraper.

118
00:10:25,000 --> 00:10:32,420
In 1829, the Tremont House in Boston became the first hotel in America with indoor plumbing.

119
00:10:33,380 --> 00:10:37,160
This was considered an enormous luxury at that moment.

120
00:10:38,020 --> 00:10:42,620
But of course, once indoor plumbing was incorporated, it became a standard.

121
00:10:43,460 --> 00:10:48,160
And when we see plumbing coming indoors at the Tremont, we see plumbing coming indoors to stay.

122
00:10:49,120 --> 00:10:53,160
By the time the first skyscrapers were constructed in the late 1880s,

123
00:10:53,740 --> 00:10:57,300
indoor plumbing had already become a common fixture of city life.

124
00:10:57,301 --> 00:11:03,800
But plumbing in a skyscraper presented unique challenges for engineers.

125
00:11:03,801 --> 00:11:09,540
The story of plumbing in skyscrapers is really the story of the development of pumps.

126
00:11:09,541 --> 00:11:16,660
Pumps that were powerful enough to push water higher and higher in buildings that grew taller and taller.

127
00:11:16,661 --> 00:11:22,780
With the help of pumps, designers found they could more efficiently control water demand and pressure.

128
00:11:22,781 --> 00:11:26,480
By filling holding tanks at the tops of their buildings,

129
00:11:26,481 --> 00:11:28,980
and letting gravity do the work.

130
00:11:30,840 --> 00:11:33,440
But the early office bathrooms weren't for everybody.

131
00:11:34,480 --> 00:11:39,220
The early skyscrapers had single bathrooms at the core,

132
00:11:39,940 --> 00:11:44,180
and people walked down the hall to use the bathroom on every floor.

133
00:11:44,780 --> 00:11:48,560
Sometimes there was a men's room on one floor and a women's room on another floor.

134
00:11:49,080 --> 00:11:52,000
Sometimes in the early skyscrapers, they were just men's rooms

135
00:11:52,001 --> 00:11:54,500
because there weren't very many women in office buildings.

136
00:11:56,480 --> 00:12:00,360
Fortunately, the Caltrans building will have both men's and women's rooms.

137
00:12:01,360 --> 00:12:05,800
The Scott Company is outfitting 52 bathrooms with all the necessary items.

138
00:12:07,560 --> 00:12:10,440
But rumors have been swirling around the site.

139
00:12:11,440 --> 00:12:16,300
A couple months into the job, found out that there's going to be some financial hardships.

140
00:12:17,100 --> 00:12:18,760
There was a crunch for money.

141
00:12:18,761 --> 00:12:20,460
worse than a crunch.

142
00:12:21,400 --> 00:12:23,860
The Scott Company, in business since 1906,

143
00:12:24,960 --> 00:12:26,740
became unable to pay its bills.

144
00:12:28,540 --> 00:12:30,340
Suppliers stopped providing equipment.

145
00:12:31,680 --> 00:12:33,840
That meant trouble for Clark Construction,

146
00:12:34,200 --> 00:12:35,180
the general contractor.

147
00:12:36,240 --> 00:12:38,300
The Scott Company handled not only the plumbing,

148
00:12:38,740 --> 00:12:41,380
but also the heating and air conditioning systems.

149
00:12:42,580 --> 00:12:45,040
It impacted their performance dramatically.

150
00:12:45,041 --> 00:12:48,840
they were unable to keep up with the schedule here on this project.

151
00:12:50,280 --> 00:12:53,780
But the schedule wasn't the only thing impacted by the cash flow problems.

152
00:12:54,280 --> 00:12:58,780
When union payments fell delinquent, a number of workers had to leave the job.

153
00:12:59,620 --> 00:13:03,300
I worked for about two months, and the company started to go belly up,

154
00:13:03,380 --> 00:13:06,360
and because we're organized through a union, we had to walk off the job.

155
00:13:07,320 --> 00:13:09,620
You know, for a lot of guys, it got real tough and real hard.

156
00:13:10,060 --> 00:13:12,360
Some guys like myself, I almost lost my house.

157
00:13:12,361 --> 00:13:16,400
the trouble started in 2002

158
00:13:16,401 --> 00:13:20,160
when the Scott company worked on a Las Vegas super hotel

159
00:13:20,161 --> 00:13:23,440
and the hotel skipped out on its bill

160
00:13:23,441 --> 00:13:28,040
a lot of the money from what I understand was being tied up on other jobs

161
00:13:28,041 --> 00:13:30,260
they were owed a lot of money

162
00:13:30,261 --> 00:13:33,020
reportedly millions

163
00:13:33,021 --> 00:13:36,740
the Scott company started running out of materials

164
00:13:36,741 --> 00:13:38,960
time and personnel

165
00:13:38,961 --> 00:13:43,180
Why isn't he working? He should be started over there.

166
00:13:43,181 --> 00:13:44,060
He needs more manpower.

167
00:13:45,540 --> 00:13:48,420
The truth comes out. He needs more. Why does he get more?

168
00:13:48,421 --> 00:13:55,960
Once the rumors got out, naturally, a lot of people started looking for other avenues for steady employment.

169
00:13:56,220 --> 00:13:58,840
When you lose key personnel, it's hard to recover.

170
00:13:59,600 --> 00:14:00,440
This flow looks clean.

171
00:14:00,441 --> 00:14:03,420
The only good thing about it, it's clean, but there's no work going on, right?

172
00:14:04,860 --> 00:14:08,240
Clark Construction stepped in to try to retain crucial people.

173
00:14:08,960 --> 00:14:13,300
Clark even paid suppliers directly, just to keep the job going.

174
00:14:14,580 --> 00:14:17,100
But Clark couldn't keep the Scott Company going.

175
00:14:19,060 --> 00:14:23,840
After a century of service, the Scott Company was forced to close its doors.

176
00:14:26,560 --> 00:14:32,180
Fortunately for the project, Clark construction was insured for events like this.

177
00:14:32,600 --> 00:14:34,680
And we'll be able to get to that shortly.

178
00:14:35,000 --> 00:14:38,380
The build team chose Western Air as the replacement plumbing contractor.

179
00:14:38,960 --> 00:14:41,220
And the transition went smoothly.

180
00:14:43,700 --> 00:14:47,280
Western Air even hired many former Scott employees who knew the project,

181
00:14:48,100 --> 00:14:56,060
including Ed, plumbing foreman, Steve, general foreman, and Joe, piping superintendent.

182
00:14:56,720 --> 00:14:58,120
I'm going to finish this job up.

183
00:14:59,900 --> 00:15:03,060
Western Air has talked to me a little bit about staying on with them.

184
00:15:03,061 --> 00:15:06,520
And like I say, they seem to be a very, very good company.

185
00:15:06,521 --> 00:15:10,920
and I will probably stay on with them if the occasion arises.

186
00:15:11,720 --> 00:15:14,480
As Clark struggles to keep everything on schedule,

187
00:15:14,840 --> 00:15:17,560
they're hoping there are no more shocking surprises,

188
00:15:18,620 --> 00:15:23,280
especially since the electricians are in next to get this building wired.

189
00:15:25,160 --> 00:15:29,740
Prior to 1881, 90% of human waste in New York City

190
00:15:29,741 --> 00:15:32,080
had to be removed from privy vaults manually

191
00:15:32,081 --> 00:15:35,440
and transported to the docks via public streets,

192
00:15:35,441 --> 00:15:37,460
posing a severe health hazard.

193
00:15:38,000 --> 00:15:40,120
Building a skyscraper, the arteries will return

194
00:15:40,121 --> 00:15:41,760
on Modern Marvels.

195
00:15:46,840 --> 00:15:48,800
Plug it in, flip a switch,

196
00:15:49,240 --> 00:15:51,740
light it up, turn it on, and crank it up.

197
00:15:52,660 --> 00:15:54,680
Electricity keeps our big buildings running.

198
00:16:00,200 --> 00:16:01,860
We don't think much about it

199
00:16:01,861 --> 00:16:05,060
because the entire infrastructure is hidden behind walls.

200
00:16:05,440 --> 00:16:19,440
But in the Caltrans building in Los Angeles, the simple task of turning on a light takes giant transformers, breakers, distribution panels, conduit, miles of wire, and

201
00:16:20,165 --> 00:16:21,840
plenty of patience.

202
00:16:25,600 --> 00:16:30,280
Broken down, it's somewhat similar to the electrical system in your house.

203
00:16:30,281 --> 00:16:37,760
At home, power travels from the panel board to the outlet through the hot wire.

204
00:16:39,520 --> 00:16:46,560
When you're using a power tool such as a drill, electricity flows through the hot wire, powering the drill.

205
00:16:47,780 --> 00:16:51,940
Then back to the panel board via the neutral wire to complete the circuit.

206
00:16:54,460 --> 00:16:59,840
The ground wire is there to provide a safe path for electricity to follow in the event of a short circuit.

207
00:17:00,280 --> 00:17:04,360
which might result from a loose hot wire in the drill.

208
00:17:07,180 --> 00:17:10,960
The electrical system of this building follows the same basic rules,

209
00:17:11,520 --> 00:17:13,400
but on a much bigger scale.

210
00:17:15,020 --> 00:17:19,180
It starts four stories below ground in the electrical vault.

211
00:17:20,540 --> 00:17:22,800
The vault is owned by the Department of Water and Power.

212
00:17:23,360 --> 00:17:26,460
The contractor actually built the vault for us to our specifications,

213
00:17:26,461 --> 00:17:30,700
But it's our equipment, our facility, and we're responsible for it.

214
00:17:31,200 --> 00:17:36,780
So only the Department of Water and Power has access to the vault for safety reasons and also because it's our equipment.

215
00:17:38,860 --> 00:17:43,100
Chris Logie with the Los Angeles DWP is in charge of setting up the vault.

216
00:17:44,620 --> 00:17:47,820
It's the first step toward getting this building turned on.

217
00:17:48,640 --> 00:17:55,300
We're installing the equipment, the transformers, the fuse cabinets, and we're just starting from scratch.

218
00:17:55,980 --> 00:18:00,100
It's certainly my biggest project to date. This is a big, big job.

219
00:18:01,280 --> 00:18:05,000
The fun begins as the first truckload of equipment arrives at the site.

220
00:18:12,600 --> 00:18:13,300
Ready!

221
00:18:13,301 --> 00:18:27,020
Today we unloaded two transformers.

222
00:18:27,760 --> 00:18:31,760
The transformers are very heavy, about 25,000 pounds, the larger one.

223
00:18:32,680 --> 00:18:35,380
There are also tons of ancillary equipment.

224
00:18:37,280 --> 00:18:41,080
Once set up, only the DWP has keys to the vault,

225
00:18:41,081 --> 00:18:44,560
meaning no one working in the building has access.

226
00:18:46,000 --> 00:18:49,160
That's because there's going to be a lot of juice running through this room.

227
00:18:51,500 --> 00:18:54,360
There's pipes that are running underground and they're all over the city

228
00:18:54,361 --> 00:18:57,220
and they're bringing in a very high voltage cable.

229
00:18:57,360 --> 00:18:59,780
It's 34,500 volts.

